o when it's your turn, the previous action is from the player to your right? Let's clarify.\n\nTypically, in a four-player game, players are seated East, South, West, North, with East as dealer sometimes, but prevailing wind is separate.\n\nHere, prevailing wind is South, so South is the prevailing direction.\n\nMy seat wind is North, so I am the North seat player.\n\nThen, the players:\n\n- Previous player: to my right, which would be East seat? Let's define.\n\nAssume seats: East, South, West, North, clockwise.\n\nPrevailing wind South, so South is prevailing.\n\nMy seat wind North, so I am North.\n\nThen, player to my left (next to play after me) is East.\n\nPlayer to my right (who just played before me) is West.\n\nPlayer across is South.\n\nIn the state:\n\n\"The game history of the next player is: Play W4.\" \u2014 next player is to my left, East seat.\n\n\"The game history of the player sitting across is: Play F4.\" \u2014 across is South, prevailing wind.\n\n\"The game history of the previous player is: Play B5.\" \u2014 previous player is to my right, West seat.\n\nSo:\n\n- Previous player (West): played B5\n\n- Across (South): played F4\n\n- Next player (East): played W4\n\nNow, previous player (West) discarded B5.\n\nB5 is Dots 5.\n\nSince they discarded B5, it might mean they don't need B5, so perhaps they are not building hands involving B5, like 4,5,6 or 5,6,7.\n\nSo B6 and B7 might be less critical for them, but could be for others.\n\nSimilarly, next player (East) discarded W4, so W4 out, so for Characters, 4,5,6 less likely.\n\nFor me, discarding B7: since West discarded B5, and B5 is close to B7, it might be safer to discard B7 because if West had B6, they might have kept B5, but they discarded it, so perhaps not.\n\nNot reliable.\n\nPerhaps discard a tile that is duplicated or something, but no.\n\nAnother pattern: I have F2:2, which is good.\n\nAlso, I have T4,T5,T6, which is a chow.\n\nIf I can get another chow, like with W or B.\n\nBut for Mixed Straight, it's good.\n\nPerhaps I can aim for \"Pure Shifted Chows\" or something, but let's stick with Mixed Straight.\n\nI think B7 is a reasonable discard.\n\nW6 is also possible.\n\nBut let's see the valid actions; Play B7 is listed.\n\nAlso, Play W6.\n\nNow, why B7 over W6?\n\nI have B1, which is for Dots 1,2,3, so B7 is not related.\n\nSimilarly, for W, I have W8,W9 for 7,8,9, so W6 is not directly related, but W6 could be part of 6,7,8 if I had W7,W8, but I need W7 for 7,8,9, so W6 not needed.\n\nSimilarly.\n\nBut B7 is Dots 7, and I have B1 for low, so B7 is high, not useful.\n\nW6 is mid, not useful.\n\nPerhaps discard T9, but it's terminal.\n\nT9 is Bamboo 9, and I have T4,T5,T6 for mid, so T9 is high terminal.\n\nDiscarding terminal is riskier.\n\nIn some cases, terminals are safer to discard late, but here early game.\n\nI think for safety, discard a simple like B7.\n\nMoreover, in the unshown, B5:3, but one played, so B5 is out, which might make B7 less desirable for some sequences.\n\nSimilarly, for W, W4 out.\n\nBut let's choose B7.\n\nAnother thing: I have F3, which is West Wind.\n\nMy seat wind is North, so F3 is not seat wind.\n\nPrevailing is South, not West.\n\nSo F3 has no special value.\n\nDiscarding F3 might be ok, but it's an honor tile, so could complete a pung for someone.\n\nSince honors are often used, discarding honor might be riskier than simple.\n\nFor example, \"All Honors\" is rare, but \"Dragon Pung\" is common.\n\nSo discarding J2 or F3 could let someone complete a dragon or wind pung.\n\nWhereas B7 is simple, less likely to complete a win immediately.\n\nSimilarly, W2 or W6.\n\nSo simples are safer to discard than honors.\n\nTherefore, among the options, B7, W6, W2 are safer than J2, F3, F2.\n\nT9 is terminal, might be medium risk.\n\nBut T9 is simple? No, T9 is terminal, so higher risk.\n\nTerminals are riskier.\n\nSo B7 (simple) is safer than T9 (terminal).\n\nSimilarly, W2 is simple, safer.\n\nW2 is Character 2, simple.\n\nB7